==================================================
Lab 2: File Path Traversal, Traversal Sequences Blocked
==================================================

=======================
WHAT THIS LAB IS ABOUT
=======================

A shopping website loads product images through a URL that includes the filename.
The server has a filter that blocks ../ sequences, so the simple payload from Lab 7 no longer works.
The filter only looks for ../, so an absolute path that starts from the root folder avoids it.

Goal: read the contents of /etc/passwd.

==============================
HOW THE WEBSITE NORMALLY WORKS
==============================

When a product image loads, the browser requests /image?filename=<name>.
The server reads the file from its image folder, /var/www/images/, and returns it.

=======================
 WHAT IS THE PROBLEM
=======================

The filter is a blocklist. It removes or rejects the traversal sequence ../, but it does not stop the filename from being an absolute path.
If the server joins the filename to the image folder in a way that keeps the absolute path, the request can read any file the web server can access.

===========================
THREE THINGS TO UNDERSTAND
===========================

Traversal sequence (../): This moves up one folder. The filter blocks it, so it cannot be used here.
Absolute path: A path that starts from the root folder, such as /etc/passwd. It does not need ../ to reach a file.
Blocklist: A filter that rejects known bad input. It only protects against the patterns it knows about, so other ways of writing the same path can get through.

======================================
THE ATTACK
======================================

Instead of a relative filename with ../, we send:

/etc/passwd

The server builds a path that roughly looks like:

/var/www/images//etc/passwd

Depending on how the server joins the path, the leading slash can replace the image folder, so the request reads /etc/passwd directly.
The response contains the contents of /etc/passwd.

============================
HOW TO SOLVE IT MANUALLY
============================

Open the lab and load any product image.
Replace the filename in the image URL with /etc/passwd.
Load the page. The text of /etc/passwd appears, and the lab is marked as solved.

=========================
WHAT THIS SCRIPT DOES
=========================

* It asks for the lab's base URL and builds the target as /image.
* It prints a message saying it is sending the absolute-path payload.
* It sends a GET request with filename set to /etc/passwd. The params argument adds it to the URL query string.
* If the server returns status 200, it prints the response text, which is the contents of /etc/passwd.
* If the request fails or the status is different, it prints an error instead.

=============================
WHY THIS HAPPENS
=============================

The filter checks for the pattern ../ but not for every way a path can leave the intended folder.
An absolute path bypasses the pattern check, because it never needs to move up a folder.

=============================
HOW TO PREVENT THIS
=============================

Do not build file paths from user input. Map each image to an ID or a fixed name on the server.
If user input must be used, resolve the full path and check that it still starts with the allowed folder.
Block at the path-check level, not by searching for specific strings, since blocklists are easy to bypass.

Other good practices:

Run the web server with only the file permissions it needs.
Keep sensitive files outside the paths the application can reach.

=============================
 KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
=============================
A blocklist that only checks for ../ does not stop absolute paths.
Check the resolved path, not the raw input.
Never trust a filename that comes from the user.
